<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>The quadratic function \(f\) is given by \(f(x)=x^2+2x+6\).</para>
    <para>Find \(f(-2)\). </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>6</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->
<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>Find \(f(0)\). </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>6</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->
<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of this quadratic function? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>-1</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->
<exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>Does the graph have any \(x\)-intercepts? Explain or show how you know. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>No, the graph does not have any \(x\)-intercepts. The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is 5 because \(f(-1)=(-1)2+2(-1)+6=5\). The coefficient of the squared term is positive, so the vertex is a minimum, which means the output of the function is never less than 5.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->
